BINARY TREE MAXIMUM PATH SUM

LeetCode 124

🟢 Problem

Given a binary tree, return the maximum path sum.

Rules:

A path can start and end at ANY node.

Must follow parent-child connections.

Path does NOT need to go through root.

Can turn at only ONE node (like a “V” shape).

🔎 Example
        -10
        /  \
       9   20
           / \
          15  7


Output:

42


Because best path is:

15 → 20 → 7


= 42

❌ Why This Is Tricky?

Two important facts:

The best path might NOT pass through root.

A node can use BOTH children in final answer.

But when returning upward, it can use only ONE child.

That difference is critical.

🧠 Key Idea

At each node:

Two possibilities:

🔹 1. Path passing through node (for global answer)
left_gain + node.val + right_gain

🔹 2. Path extending upward (return value)

Must choose only ONE side:

node.val + max(left_gain, right_gain)

🟢 Define Function
dfs(node) → maximum gain from this node upward


Also maintain:

globalMax

🟢 Rules

If child gives negative gain → ignore it.

Because negative path reduces total.

So:

left = max(0, dfs(node.left))
right = max(0, dfs(node.right))

🟢 Core Formula

Update global answer:

globalMax = max(globalMax, left + right + node.val)


Return to parent:

return node.val + max(left, right)

🟢 Java Code
class Solution {

    int globalMax = Integer.MIN_VALUE;

    public int maxPathSum(TreeNode root) {
        dfs(root);
        return globalMax;
    }

    private int dfs(TreeNode node) {

        if (node == null) return 0;

        int left = Math.max(0, dfs(node.left));
        int right = Math.max(0, dfs(node.right));

        int currentPath = left + right + node.val;

        globalMax = Math.max(globalMax, currentPath);

        return node.val + Math.max(left, right);
    }
}

🟢 Why max(0, child)?

If:

child_gain = -5


Taking it reduces path.

Better to ignore it.

🟢 Dry Run (Important)

For node 20:

Left = 15
Right = 7

Update:

15 + 7 + 20 = 42


Return:

20 + max(15,7) = 35


Then for root -10:

Left = 9
Right = 35

Check:

9 + 35 - 10 = 34


Not greater than 42.

So answer remains 42.

🟢 Time Complexity
O(n)


Every node visited once.

🟢 Why This Question Is Powerful?

It teaches:

Tree DP

Postorder traversal

Local vs Global decision

Ignoring negatives

Maintaining global state

Most candidates fail because they:

❌ Try to return both children upward
❌ Don’t handle negative values
❌ Forget to maintain global variable

